<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/scenes/level5-distribution-functions> {
  ex:l5-d08a-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:probability-mass-function ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-d08a-heading, ex:l5-d08a-plot, ex:l5-d08a-math ; cd:narration "D08a. Continue from the count models instead of restarting with abstract definitions. Each binomial bar is the probability of exactly one integer count. The highlighted count two has probability 0.301989888, about 30.20%. All eleven masses from zero to ten sum to one. The Poisson model also has a mass function, but its complete support has no finite upper count. These are theoretical model probabilities, not the simulated series frequencies shown earlier. Probability mass function is abbreviated PMF. Bar height is already probability; no density-width calculation is needed. The full plot, result and complete symbol legend are visible immediately."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-d08b-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:cumulative-distribution-function ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-d08b-heading, ex:l5-d08b-plot, ex:l5-d08b-math ; cd:narration "D08b. The cumulative distribution function, CDF, adds all mass at or below a real threshold. At two, zero, one and two positives contribute, giving 0.6777995264. This differs from the individual mass 0.301989888 at two. The graph must be a right-continuous staircase, not diagonal interpolation between counts. Closed points give the function value at a threshold; open points mark the left limit before a jump. Between integer counts the CDF is constant, below zero it is zero and from ten upwards it is one. A theoretical CDF is determined by the model and is not an empirical CDF made from observed data. No Level-4 ECDF lesson or sample-quantile algorithm is repeated here."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-d08c-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:probability-density-function ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-d08c-heading, ex:l5-d08c-plot, ex:l5-d08c-math ; cd:narration "Continuous quantities need density areas rather than count masses. This is a tool for later models of concentrations and estimators, not a fitted measurement distribution or a claim that nitrate errors follow this triangular shape. The constructed triangle spans 9.5 to 10.5 mg/L, is zero elsewhere and has peak 2 L/mg at 10 mg/L. Its total area is one. The interval 9.75 to 10.25 mg/L has area 0.75. Density height is not point probability and may exceed one. Reciprocal concentration units multiplied by concentration width give dimensionless probability. An exact real value has point probability zero, while a rounded measurement result such as 10.00 mg/L represents a finite reporting interval. This probability range is not a confidence interval for an unknown population mean. The next slides connect areas to CDF differences and quantiles, then place the normal model within the nitrate investigation."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-d08d-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:cumulative-distribution-function ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-d08d-heading, ex:l5-d08d-plot, ex:l5-d08d-math ; cd:narration "D08d. Use the same constructed continuous model and the same interval as on the density slide. Integrating its density gives a continuous CDF. At 9.75 mg/L it is 0.125; at 10.25 mg/L it is 0.875. The difference is 0.75, matching the shaded density area. The CDF is sampled at exact values for drawing; line segments approximate its curved shape between those samples. The marked probabilities and interval calculation are exact for the constructed model. No CDF area is shaded: probability comes from a difference in CDF heights, not area under the CDF. Including or excluding an individual interval endpoint makes no difference here because the continuous model has no point masses. For a discrete model, subtracting F(a) excludes mass at a; an inclusive lower endpoint instead needs the left limit. Do not transfer the continuous identity to a discrete interval unchanged."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-d08e-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:theoretical-quantile ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-d08e-heading, ex:l5-d08e-plot, ex:l5-d08e-math ; cd:narration "D08e. A CDF maps a threshold to cumulative probability. A theoretical quantile reverses that direction: start with a requested probability and take the first threshold where the CDF reaches or exceeds it. The infimum definition works for both continuous and discrete models. In the binomial example, the CDF at one is 0.3758096384 and at two it is 0.6777995264, so the 0.5 quantile is two. The probability level is crossed by a jump, so the CDF at that quantile is not exactly 0.5. The horizontal reference is a requested probability, not another observed dataset. This is a theoretical model quantile, not a revisit of empirical R quantile types. A quantile has the result's units: counts here and concentration units for a concentration model. This completes the mass, density, CDF and quantile distinction before introducing normal measurement errors."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-d08a-heading a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-d08a-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
